# Resource Tracks

Characters have three aspects: **Mind**, **Body**, and **Soul**. Each aspect has a
Resilience trait and two resource tracks: one slow-recovering track and one
fast-recovering track. **Mind resources support magic** (Spirit and Mana); **Soul resources track social/identity endurance** (Plasticity and Attention). Body remains physical.

| Aspect | Resilience | Slow track | Fast track |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Mind | **Will** | **Spirit** | **Mana** |
| Body | **Health** | **Injury** | **Fatigue** |
| Soul | **Conviction** | **Plasticity** | **Attention** |

The Resilience trait for an aspect determines the number of slots at **each
severity** on both of that aspect's tracks. It also supplies the *unmodified* Effect threshold. Armor and other relevant protective effects can modify the **effective threshold for injury** without altering the number of available mark slots; see [[Project Framework]].

## Severity Grades

Resource marks have four grades:

| Grade | Penalty |
| --- | ---: |
| Minor | 0 |
| Middling | -1 |
| Major | -2 |
| Final | -4 |

**Injury** calls its Final grade **Fatal**.

For each track, apply only the penalty from its highest occupied grade. Multiple
marks at the same grade do not increase that track's penalty. Penalties from
different tracks stack.

For example, a character with a Final-or-Fatal mark on all six tracks suffers
-24 to rolls.

## Applying Marks

When a successfully applied Effect produces a magnitude against a personal resource, compare that
magnitude to its effective threshold, R (normally the relevant Resilience; protective equipment may adjust the injury threshold, **not** mark capacity):

- magnitude 0: no mark;
- positive magnitude less than R: one Minor mark;
- R to less than 2 x R: one Middling mark;
- 2 x R to less than 3 x R: one Major mark;
- 3 x R to less than 4 x R: one Final mark;
- each additional full multiple of R adds another Final mark.

A single sufficiently large incident can therefore inflict several Final marks at
once.

For example, against Health 4, an Injury Effect of 1-3 causes a Minor Injury,
4-7 a Middling Injury, 8-11 a Major Injury, 12-15 one Fatal Injury, 16-19 two
Fatal Injuries, and 20-23 three Fatal Injuries. An Effect of 24 or more fills all
four Fatal slots from an otherwise uninjured state and kills the character.

**Shared Project rule:** Non-character Projects use these same four mark grades and promotion rules, but their **Scope** supplies the number of slots at each grade, and **Recalcitrance** supplies the grade threshold. Personal Injury/Mana/etc. retain Resilience-sized capacity even where armor raises the effective injury threshold. A Project is complete when its Final slots are full.

## Promotion

If every slot at a grade is already filled and another mark of that grade is
applied, clear all marks at that grade and add one mark at the next higher grade.
Promotion can cascade.

Final is the top grade and does not promote further. When all Final slots on a
track are filled, that track is **exhausted**.

## Universal fast-to-slow exhaustion routing

**Once an aspect's fast-recovery track is already exhausted, any subsequent
effect (including a self-inflicted Cost) that would normally mark that fast
track instead marks the same aspect's slow-recovery track.** This applies
universally: **Fatigue → Injury** (Body), **Mana → Spirit** (Mind), and
**Attention → Plasticity** (Soul).

Resolve each effect as a **single incident**. First check whether its **named
fast target is exhausted at the start of the incident**; if so, change the
target to the associated slow track *before assigning marks*. Keep the
effect's rolled magnitude and Tier conversion, but determine grade from the
**effective severity threshold appropriate to the actual destination**.
Apply normal mark promotion and slow-track capacity. Never add the same
incident to both tracks, divide its marks between them, or automatically
add a Minor slow mark merely because the fast track became exhausted during
that incident. The **next** fast-targeted incident spills into slow.

The rule applies to ordinary attacks, hazards, social combat, spellcasting
Costs, and other resource Effects. A redirected effect **does not count as
directly targeting** its slow destination: a mundane social attack still
targets Attention, yet once Attention is exhausted later mundane
Attention-targeting Effects damage Plasticity by overflow. This does not
grant mundane attacks permission to target Plasticity *directly*; that still
requires a supernatural attack.

If the fast track recovers at least one Final slot, later fast-targeted
incidents again affect fast normally. Recovery continues on each track
according to its own cadence. Exhausting the slow track triggers the
aspect's slow-exhaustion consequences, which are handled separately from
this redirection rule. Non-personal Projects are not paired resources and
do not acquire overflow by default.

## Supernatural Healing and Restoration

**General rule across Geard Lif, not a setting-specific spell rule:** When a legitimate supernatural Effect **restores an existing personal resource track**, resolve its Application as the Effect specifies, roll normal Potence, and determine the grade and number of *restorative marks* using the **target's base aspect Resilience** (Health for Injury/Fatigue, Will for Spirit/Mana, Conviction for Plasticity/Attention) as the Effect severity threshold. Potence zero produces no restoration; higher Potence may produce multiple Final-grade restorative marks. Protection that raises incoming Injury severity (armor, wards, flesh spells) does **not** raise the patient's healing threshold or alter their resource capacity. When Tiers differ, apply ordinary Tier conversion to Potence before grading. Powers may specify narrower legal resources or special restrictions.

**At the table:** First cancel one existing resource mark **of the same severity** for each restorative mark. If there is no matching mark, use the *equivalent value of lower-grade marks*, or reduce a higher-grade mark and return the unused value in lower-grade marks, following the normal promotion relationships. Stop at an empty track; restoration never creates negative marks. One higher-grade mark is worth **R + 1** marks of the immediately lower grade, where **R is the target track's mark capacity per grade**. For Health 2, **one Major = three Middling = nine Minor**; one Final = three Major. Resolve multiple Final restorative marks separately, but the arithmetic result is the same as subtracting their combined value.

**Precise invariant, for adjudication or software:** Let the target track hold mark counts `m[0]` through `m[3]` in grades Minor/Middling/Major/Final, let `b = R + 1`, and define

`V = m[0] + b*m[1] + b^2*m[2] + b^3*m[3]`.

A successful healing Effect with restorative grade `g` and count `n` removes `H = n*b^g` infliction-equivalent Minor marks: `V' = max(0, V - H)`. Rewrite `V'` in base `b` as the four normal mark counts (each 0 through R), retaining the existing graded track and penalties. This is the exact **inverse of infliction/promotion accounting**, independent of which wounds were inflicted first or what mixed grades remain. For a Health-2 track, (Minor 1, Middling 2, Major 1) has value `1 + 2*3 + 9 = 16`; one Major restorative mark removes 9, leaving value 7 = **Minor 1, Middling 2, Major 0**. A Final wound worth 27 minus one Major healing mark worth 9 leaves **two Major wounds**.

**This is not ordinary timed recovery.** Natural recovery below still prioritizes the lowest occupied grade and operates in discrete reverse-promotion steps. A Major wound at Health 2 is worth **nine Minor marks when inflicted or supernaturally healed**, but takes **seven ordinary recovery steps** to clear when isolated. These two measures have different purposes and should not be interchanged. Healing may be more effective at removing an existing high-grade wound than running an equal number of natural recovery steps.

**Limits:** Restore the **named damaged track**; do not automatically undo Injury/Spirit/Plasticity that resulted from an earlier fast-to-slow overflow by healing only Fatigue/Mana/Attention. **Ordinary restoration is not resurrection:** a character whose Injury track is exhausted is dead and requires an explicitly defined resurrection effect, not a generic healing Effect. Nothing here creates new recovery-per-turn actions, heals Projects automatically, or changes the normal recovery timetable. Other exhausted-resource consequences may need special conditions if specified by their Domain.

## Recovery

A recovery step always works on the lowest occupied grade.

- If Minor marks are present, clear one Minor mark.
- Otherwise, remove one Middling mark and fill all Minor slots.
- Otherwise, remove one Major mark and fill all Middling slots.
- Otherwise, remove one Final mark and fill all Major slots.

Fast tracks take one recovery step **per action**. Slow tracks take one recovery
step **per day**.

## Exhaustion

Exhausting a fast track incapacitates the character in the relevant domain
(unconsciousness for appropriate physical or mental cases; social incapacity
need not literally cause unconsciousness). **Further fast-targeted Effects
spill to the associated slow track** by the rule above; exhaustion itself
does **not** automatically inflict an additional Minor slow mark.

Exhausting **Injury** kills the character. The catastrophic consequences of
exhausting Spirit (Mind) or Plasticity (Soul) remain to be defined.

## Ordinary Uses

Some ordinary actions interact with these tracks directly:

- mundane weapons normally attack **Injury**;
- mundane exertion costs **Fatigue**;
- socially and psychologically demanding engagement can cost **Attention**;
- direct **Plasticity** targeting requires **supernatural** effects, but mundane social attacks targeting Attention **overflow into Plasticity** after Attention exhaustion; reputation and relationship consequences can also be handled through fiction and Projects;
- magical exertion usually costs **Mana**, with lasting supernatural damage on **Spirit**;
- supernatural Domains define their own resource costs and interactions.

## Costs

A resource **Cost** uses the same Effect resolution as any other Power.

A Cost has a rating and a target resource. Roll the Cost rating; there is no
Application roll. Nothing reduces the Cost rating before it is rolled: armor,
external defenses, and other mitigation do not discount it. The user's Resilience **does not reduce the number of Cost dice rolled**. The target aspect's Resilience **always sets the resource mark capacity**; the **effective static severity threshold** is ordinarily that same Resilience, but a setting can define an appropriate resistance Attribute/Skill and legal protective gear that instead increase or otherwise set the effective Cost threshold. This is **not** a reduction to Cost dice, is **not** an extra resistance roll, and never adds capacity slots.

Apply those marks to the target resource normally, checking **fast-track
exhaustion before each Cost**. If its fast target is already exhausted,
apply the *same rolled Cost magnitude* to the paired slow track instead,
using the slow track's appropriate effective severity threshold (which
need not inherit every fast-targeted cost-reduction bonus).

A Cost roll can produce zero magnitude, in which case it inflicts no mark.

Tier applies to Cost rolls in the same way it applies to other Effect rolls. The
exact Tier mechanics are still under development.
